(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pharm-e3-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs — Clinical Vignettes 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 56-year-old man who works outdoors as a landscaper is started on hydrochlorothiazide. Which counseling point is most relevant to his work?","choices":["Use sun protection because of photosensitivity","Report ringing in the ears right away","Avoid foods that contain any potassium","Expect breast tenderness over the next weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Photosensitivity and skin rashes are listed adverse effects of thiazides, so sun exposure matters for an outdoor worker. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man admitted with decompensated heart failure is on a furosemide infusion but remains volume overloaded. His creatinine clearance is very low. The team wants to add the thiazide-type drug classically chosen when kidney function is this poor. Which drug should be added?","choices":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Metolazone","Chlorothiazide","Indapamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Metolazone is the thiazide-type drug singled out as effective at low creatinine clearance, where the other thiazides are classically less effective. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman takes hydrochlorothiazide for hypertension. Routine laboratory tests show a slightly raised serum calcium, and she has no symptoms. Which is the most likely cause of this finding?","choices":["Less calcium excreted by the kidney","More calcium lost from the kidney","A rise in the heart's calcium stores","Volume overload from sodium retention"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides decrease renal calcium excretion by increasing calcium reabsorption, so serum calcium can rise a little. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with heart failure takes furosemide and has a persistently low serum potassium. Which drug can be added to help him hold on to potassium?","choices":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Acetazolamide","Bumetanide","Amiloride"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Potassium-sparing diuretics are most often combined with other diuretics, and they offset the potassium loss of loops. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman recently started a drug for heart failure and reports transient patches of brightness in part of her visual field that settle on their own. She has no nausea and no color change in her vision. Which drug is most likely responsible?","choices":["Digoxin","Sacubitril-valsartan","Ivabradine","Dapagliflozin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ivabradine affects retinal photoreceptors and causes transient brightness in a limited area of the visual field, called phosphenes. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man starts digoxin for heart failure. Which cellular action increases the force of his heart's contraction?","choices":["Inhibition of phosphodiesterase III, raising cyclic adenosine monophosphate","Block of the pacemaker current in the sinoatrial node","Stimulation of beta-1 receptors in the myocardium","Inhibition of the sodium-potassium pump, raising calcium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blocking the sodium-potassium pump raises intracellular sodium, which raises intracellular calcium through the sodium-calcium exchanger and strengthens contraction. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with heart failure is told that his angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor will slow the progression of his disease. Which effects underlie this benefit?","choices":["Lower preload, afterload and ventricular remodeling","Higher contractility with a faster heart rate","Block of the sodium-potassium pump in myocytes","More sodium retention to raise preload"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These drugs decrease preload, afterload and sympathetic activation, and reduce left ventricular hypertrophy, dilation and remodeling. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man's heart failure was stabilized by a short course of intravenous milrinone. The team asks whether he should go home on long-term milrinone. Which concern argues against it?","choices":["Higher mortality and morbidity than placebo","Severe hyperkalemia from potassium retention","Marked bradycardia from slowed nodal conduction","Breast enlargement from androgen receptor effects"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Milrinone is approved only for short-term use, because long-term use is associated with higher mortality and morbidity than placebo. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with prediabetes starts hydrochlorothiazide for hypertension. Which metabolic effect should be followed?","choices":["A fall in blood glucose","A fall in low-density lipoprotein cholesterol","A fall in serum uric acid","A rise in blood glucose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides decrease glucose tolerance and insulin secretion, so hyperglycemia is a listed adverse effect to follow. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman takes triamterene-hydrochlorothiazide and was told to cut her sodium intake. She now seasons food with a salt substitute made of potassium chloride. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Use more substitute to replace lost potassium","Take a potassium tablet with each dose","Drink extra water to dilute the potassium","Do not use potassium-based salt substitutes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Potassium-sparing diuretics hold potassium, and potassium supplements are a listed caution, so added dietary potassium risks hyperkalemia. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has just been switched from lisinopril to sacubitril-valsartan for heart failure. Which pair of adverse effects should be watched for most?","choices":["Low blood pressure and high potassium","Low potassium and high calcium","Low platelets and ventricular arrhythmia","Yellow vision and slow pulse"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypotension, hyperkalemia, cough and renal insufficiency are the most common adverse effects of sacubitril-valsartan. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with heart failure takes lisinopril, carvedilol, furosemide and digoxin. Over three weeks he develops a persistent dry cough that interferes with sleep, with no fever, wheeze or sputum. Which of his medicines is the most likely cause?","choices":["Carvedilol","Lisinopril","Furosemide","Digoxin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cough is a listed problem of angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, along with angioedema, hypotension, raised potassium and impaired renal function. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer has symptomatic hypercalcemia and is being given intravenous saline. Which diuretic is added to increase her calcium excretion?","choices":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Amiloride","Furosemide","Acetazolamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics increase calcium and magnesium excretion, so furosemide can be added alongside saline, which remains the main treatment. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman takes acetazolamide for glaucoma. Blood tests show a low serum bicarbonate and a low pH, and she feels drowsy. Which acid-base disturbance is this?","choices":["Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors cause bicarbonate loss and metabolic acidosis, along with potassium depletion and drowsiness. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man is admitted with acute decompensated heart failure and marked hypertension. He is cold and congested. Which intravenous drug adds contractility and also lowers afterload by balanced arterial and venous dilation?","choices":["Dobutamine","Milrinone","Digoxin","Ivabradine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Milrinone is an inotrope and vasodilator, so it raises cardiac output while decreasing afterload. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with stable heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is about to start a beta blocker. Which approach to starting it is most appropriate?","choices":["Start a full dose to gain the benefit quickly","Start it during an acute decompensation","Avoid it because it is contraindicated in heart failure","Start very low and increase slowly while watching for worsening"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are started at very low doses and titrated up slowly over weeks while monitoring for worsening heart failure signs. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with heart failure takes lisinopril and is symptomatic. His cardiologist plans to change him to sacubitril-valsartan. Which step is required?","choices":["Stop lisinopril and allow a 36-hour washout","Continue both drugs for added synergy","Stop lisinopril and start the new drug at once","Stop his beta blocker and keep lisinopril"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sacubitril-valsartan must not be given with an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor, and a 36-hour washout avoids overlapping adverse effects. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with chronic kidney disease has a creatinine clearance of about 25 mL/min and new leg edema. Which diuretic class is expected to remain effective at this level of kidney function?","choices":["A typical thiazide","A carbonic anhydrase inhibitor","A potassium-sparing diuretic","A loop diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics remain effective when creatinine clearance falls below 30 mL/min, which is why they are used in renal insufficiency. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with cirrhosis and ascites starts furosemide. Four days later he reports muscle weakness and palpitations. Which laboratory abnormality best explains his symptoms?","choices":["High serum potassium","High serum calcium","Low serum potassium","High serum magnesium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics increase potassium excretion, and the resulting hypokalemia can cause weakness and cardiac arrhythmias. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with stable heart failure with reduced ejection fraction needs a beta blocker that has been shown to lower mortality. Which drug should be chosen?","choices":["Atenolol","Propranolol","Carvedilol","Nadolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Carvedilol, metoprolol succinate and bisoprolol are the beta blockers with a mortality benefit in heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with heart failure takes a loop diuretic. She is admitted with a serious infection and treated with gentamicin. On day five she reports ringing in the ears and muffled hearing. Which interaction most likely explains this?","choices":["Gentamicin adding to the diuretic's cochlear toxicity","A blunted diuretic response from prostaglandin blockade","Displacement of warfarin from plasma proteins","Reduced lithium clearance with lithium toxicity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycosides such as gentamicin potentiate the ototoxicity of loop diuretics, which can damage the cochlea. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has newly diagnosed heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, mild symptoms and no edema. Which drug combination should he take regardless of symptoms to prolong survival?","choices":["Loop diuretic plus digoxin to relieve his symptoms","Thiazide diuretic plus digoxin to control any edema","Loop diuretic plus thiazide diuretic combined","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor plus beta blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients should be on an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor and a beta blocker irrespective of symptoms, because both decrease mortality. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension has exertional breathlessness and edema. Echocardiography shows a thick left ventricular wall and a normal ejection fraction. Which best describes the underlying problem?","choices":["Loss of muscle mass with weak contraction","Impaired relaxation of a thick, stiff ventricle","A dilated ventricle with a low ejection fraction","Excess sodium retention with a normal heart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diastolic dysfunction is impaired relaxation of a hypertrophied, stiffer ventricle, with decreased filling and symptoms despite a preserved ejection fraction. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with heart failure takes digoxin and furosemide. He has had days of vomiting and poor intake. Which electrolyte disturbance most increases his risk of digoxin toxicity?","choices":["Low calcium","High magnesium","Low potassium","High sodium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics cause hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia, and with digitalis these provoke arrhythmias. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with advanced heart failure is being considered for spironolactone. His serum potassium is above 5 mEq/L and his serum creatinine is above 2.5. Which decision is most appropriate?","choices":["Start it because survival benefit outweighs risk","Start it together with a potassium supplement","Withhold it because of hyperkalemia risk","Start it and stop the other heart failure drugs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients are not eligible for spironolactone if potassium is above 5 or creatinine is above 2.5, because hyperkalemia and arrhythmia become likely. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with digoxin toxicity has life-threatening ventricular arrhythmias. Which treatment rapidly reverses the toxicity?","choices":["Intravenous milrinone","Intravenous furosemide","Digoxin immune Fab","Oral spironolactone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This antibody fragment, made by immunizing sheep, binds digoxin with higher affinity than the sodium-potassium pump and rapidly reverses toxicity. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman takes spironolactone for primary aldosteronism and develops irregular menstrual periods and breast tenderness. Which property of the drug best explains this?","choices":["Block of luminal sodium channels","Blockade of androgen receptors","Inhibition of carbonic anhydrase","Inhibition of the sodium-potassium-chloride carrier"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone is a steroid-like drug that also binds androgen receptors and blocks them, causing menstrual irregularities in women and gynecomastia in men; eplerenone has less effect on these receptors. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction remains symptomatic on optimal doses of an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor, a beta blocker and a diuretic. Which outcome should he expect from adding digoxin?","choices":["Longer survival and slower disease progression","Fewer symptoms, but no survival gain","Reversal of left ventricular remodeling","Lower mortality than a beta blocker gives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin improves symptoms, exercise tolerance and quality of life and cuts hospitalizations, but it has no survival benefit. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 66"}]};
